# .roles — standing constraints for all agent missions on this project

Project: Reaction Wheel Attitude Control Simulator (software-only)
Applies to: every task, mission, or prompt given to any agent (Gemini, Claude, or GPT-OSS)
inside Google Antigravity for this repository, for the entire lifetime of the project.

If any instruction in a task prompt conflicts with this file, this file wins. If an agent
believes a rule here is blocking legitimate progress, it must stop and explain the conflict
in its Artifact/summary rather than silently overriding the rule.

---

## 1. Physics integrity (non-negotiable)

R1.1 — The relationship between wheel speed and body speed (ω2 as a function of ω1) must
NEVER be computed by direct algebraic substitution of the steady-state conservation formula
(e.g. `omega2 = -(I1/I2) * omega1`) applied every frame. It must always emerge from numerically
integrating torque equations over time. Direct algebraic substitution is a forbidden shortcut:
it produces a visually similar result in the simplest case but silently breaks disturbance
response, friction modeling, and saturation behavior, and defeats the entire purpose of the
simulation. Any agent output that does this must be rejected and rewritten.

R1.2 — Physics integration must use RK4 (4th-order Runge-Kutta) or an equivalently accurate
method. Plain forward Euler integration is forbidden for the core physics engine because it
accumulates visible energy/momentum drift, which would misrepresent the conservation law this
project exists to demonstrate.

R1.3 — All internal physics and control computation must use SI units exclusively
(kilograms, meters, radians, radians/second, newton-meters, seconds). Conversion to RPM,
degrees, or other display units happens only at the UI display boundary — never inside
`core/physics`, `core/control`, `core/actuator`, or `core/sensor`.

R1.4 — Every physical constant (inertias, motor time constants, noise sigma, friction
coefficients, etc.) must be a named, documented constant with a comment stating its unit and
whether it is a "measured value" or a "representative placeholder pending real hardware
characterization." No unnamed numeric literals in physics/control code.

## 2. Architectural boundaries (non-negotiable)

R2.1 — The controller (`core/control/pidController.ts`) must never receive ground-truth state
directly. It only ever receives the sensor/estimator's output. Any code path that lets
`trueState` reach the controller's `update()` call is a bug and must be fixed immediately,
regardless of what phase of the plan is in progress.

R2.2 — `core/control/pidController.ts` must have zero imports from any UI, rendering, or
sim-orchestration module. It must remain framework-agnostic, pure, and directly portable to
embedded C++ with minimal syntax translation. Do not introduce React state, DOM references,
or async/Promise-based logic into this file.

R2.3 — The UI layer (`src/ui/**`) may only call the public API of `SimulationLoop`. It must
never import `physicsEngine.ts`, `pidController.ts`, `motorModel.ts`, `sensorModel.ts`, or
`estimator.ts` directly. If the UI needs new data exposed, add a read-only getter to
`SimulationLoop`'s public API — do not reach around it.

R2.4 — The sensor model (`core/sensor/**`) must use a seedable pseudo-random number generator
for all injected noise. Direct use of `Math.random()` anywhere in `core/sensor/` is forbidden,
because it breaks deterministic, reproducible tests.

R2.5 — Physics stepping must run on a fixed internal timestep via an accumulator pattern,
fully decoupled from render framerate (`requestAnimationFrame` rate). Never derive the physics
`dt` from the render loop's frame delta.

## 3. Testing discipline

R3.1 — No phase is complete until its stated automated tests exist and pass when run via
`npm test`. A written summary claiming "this should work" without a passing test is not
acceptable completion evidence.

R3.2 — Tests for the closed-loop controller (Phase 3 onward) must exercise the real physics
engine end-to-end, not a mocked or simplified stand-in, unless a test is explicitly and
narrowly scoped to test the controller math in isolation (and is clearly named as such, e.g.
`pidController.unit.test.ts`).

R3.3 — Any test involving randomness (sensor noise) must run with a fixed seed so results are
reproducible across runs and across machines.

## 4. Scope discipline

R4.1 — Each phase in `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` has an explicit file/folder scope. An agent
mission must not modify files outside that scope without flagging it clearly in its summary
and explaining why it was necessary.

R4.2 — Do not implement Phase 7 (3D visualization) or any other stretch/optional item before
all of Phase 0–6's acceptance criteria (PRD §9) are met and verified by the project owner.
Gold-plating an optional feature while core integration is unverified is explicitly the wrong
order of operations for this project.

R4.3 — Do not add a backend, database, authentication, or any server-side component. This is
a fully client-side, static, software-only simulation. If a task seems to require a backend,
stop and flag it rather than adding one.

R4.4 — Do not add dependencies beyond what's needed. No heavyweight charting/animation/state
libraries for what a small hand-rolled canvas module or a single lightweight library can do.
Justify any new dependency in the mission summary.

## 5. Honesty about limitations

R5.1 — Any default parameter value that is a placeholder (not yet measured from real
hardware) must be labeled as such in code comments and in the README, per PRD §10. Do not
present placeholder values as if they were validated measurements.

R5.2 — If a requested feature genuinely cannot be done within the current architecture without
violating R2.1–R2.3, the agent must say so explicitly and propose an architecture-preserving
alternative, rather than quietly bending the boundary rules to satisfy the immediate request.

## 6. Review gates

R6.1 — Phases 1 and 4 (physics engine + full loop integration) are designated
physics-integrity-critical. Agents should explicitly call out, in their summary, how the
implementation satisfies R1.1 and R2.1 for these phases specifically, so the human reviewer
can verify quickly without re-reading the entire diff line by line.

R6.2 — Every mission's Artifact/summary must include: what was implemented, what was
deliberately left out of scope, which tests were added/run and their results, and any
deviation from this file or from `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` with justification.
